templ s3BasicUsage() {
	@docLead() {
		A client needs an endpoint, a region and an access key pair. Breadboard provides all of them in the environment, so the code below, from the s3/objects-basics starter, has one S3 setting of its own: path-style addressing.
	}
	@docH2("connect", "Creating a client")
	@docCode("cmd/objects/main.go", s3ClientCode)
	@docP() {
		@docCodeWord("config.LoadDefaultConfig") builds an @docCodeWord("aws.Config") from the environment. It reads the region from @docCodeWord("AWS_REGION") and the key pair from @docCodeWord("AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID") and @docCodeWord("AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY")&#46; @docCodeWord("s3.NewFromConfig") then looks for an endpoint override, and the SDK checks the variable named after the service:
	}
	@docCode("aws-sdk-go-v2/config/env_config.go", s3EndpointEnvCode)
	@docP() {
		The S3 client's SDK ID is "S3", so the variable is @docCodeWord("AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_S3"), which Breadboard sets to the service's HTTPS address. Without it, the client would send requests to Amazon's endpoints.
	}
	@docP() {
		Nothing is sent yet. On each call, the client builds an HTTP request and signs it with Signature Version 4: it derives a signing key from the secret key, the date, the region and the service name, then computes an HMAC-SHA256 over the method, path, query, chosen headers and a hash of the body. The request carries the access key id and the signature, never the secret. The server holds the same secret, repeats the calculation, and rejects the request if the signatures differ.
	}
	@docCode("GET, as signed by the client", s3SignedHeadersCode)
	@docP() {
		The connection is HTTPS. The SDK uses Go's default TLS settings, which verify the server's certificate against the system roots. On Linux, Go reads those roots from the file @docCodeWord("SSL_CERT_FILE") names, and in a Breadboard container that is a bundle including the lab CA, so verification works with no certificate code.
	}
	@docH2("addressing", "Path-style addressing")
	@docP() {
		The S3 API has two ways to name a bucket in a URL. Virtual-hosted style puts it in the host name; path style puts it in the first path segment. By default the SDK uses virtual-hosted style whenever the bucket name allows it. This is the same GetObject in both styles:
	}
	@docCode("the request line and Host header", s3WireCode)
	@docP() {
		Virtual-hosted style needs a DNS name for every bucket. The lab resolves @docCodeWord("s3-compatible.bb.internal") but not names under it, so the starter sets @docCodeWord("UsePathStyle") and every request goes to the one host Breadboard registered.
	}
	@docH2("bucket", "Creating a bucket")
	@docCode("cmd/objects/main.go", s3BucketCode)
	@docP() {
		CreateBucket is an HTTP PUT on the bucket's path. If the bucket already exists and belongs to the same identity, the server returns @docCodeWord("BucketAlreadyOwnedByYou"); the starter treats that as success, so running it twice does not fail.
	}
	@docH2("objects", "Writing and reading objects")
	@docCode("cmd/objects/main.go", s3PutCode)
	@docP() {
		PutObject is an HTTP PUT of the whole body to the key's path. Writing to an existing key replaces the object; there is no append. Over HTTPS the SDK does not hash the body before sending it: it marks the payload as unsigned, streams it in aws-chunked encoding, and sends a CRC32 checksum of the body as a trailer after the data.
	}
	@docP() {
		Reading is GetObject. The starter does not read its notes back, but it is one call, and @docCodeWord("out.Body") streams directly from the HTTP response rather than buffering the object in memory. Always drain and close it so Go's HTTP transport can reuse the underlying connection:
	}
	@docCode("reading an object", s3GetCode)
	@docCallout("Errors are typed") {
		A missing key comes back as @docCodeWord("*types.NoSuchKey") and an existing bucket as @docCodeWord("*types.BucketAlreadyOwnedByYou"), both in the @docCodeWord("service/s3/types") package. Match them with @docCodeWord("errors.As"), as the starter does.
	}
	@docH2("run", "Run it")
	@docTerminal("bb init s3-lab", "bb starter s3/objects-basics", "bb up", "bb logs objects -f")
}

templ s3Keys() {
	@docLead() {
		A bucket holds objects under keys, and that is all the structure there is. Folders are something listing produces on the fly from the characters in the keys.
	}
	@docH2("flat", "One flat keyspace")
	@docP() {
		A key is any UTF-8 string up to 1,024 bytes. @docCodeWord("notes/2026/jan.txt") is one name with two slashes in it, not a file inside two directories. Nothing called @docCodeWord("notes/") or @docCodeWord("notes/2026/") exists unless someone writes an object with that exact key.
	}
	@docList(
		"There is no mkdir: writing notes/2026/jan.txt is enough, and nothing has to exist first.",
		"Renaming a folder means copying every object under it to a new key and deleting the old ones.",
		"Keys are listed in lexicographic order of their UTF-8 bytes, which is why a shared prefix keeps related keys together.",
	)
	@docH2("delimiter", "Prefix and delimiter")
	@docP() {
		ListObjectsV2 takes a @docCodeWord("Prefix"), which keeps only keys that start with it, and a @docCodeWord("Delimiter"), which groups them. The starter lists one level under @docCodeWord("notes/"):
	}
	@docCode("cmd/objects/main.go", s3ListCode)
	@docCode("bb logs objects (timestamps trimmed)", s3ListOutput)
	@docP() {
		What actually happens: the server walks the keys that start with @docCodeWord("notes/")&#46; For each one it looks for the delimiter after the prefix. A key with no further @docCodeWord("/"), like @docCodeWord("notes/readme.txt"), is returned in @docCodeWord("Contents")&#46; Keys that do have one are cut at that point and rolled up, so @docCodeWord("notes/2026/jan.txt") and @docCodeWord("notes/2026/feb.txt") become a single entry, @docCodeWord("notes/2026/"), in @docCodeWord("CommonPrefixes")&#46; Rolled-up keys are not returned anywhere else.
	}
	@docP() {
		Without a delimiter the same call returns all three keys, at every depth. To go one level down, repeat the call with the common prefix as the new prefix.
	}
	@docH2("pages", "Pages of 1,000")
	@docP() {
		One ListObjectsV2 response holds at most 1,000 entries, and each common prefix counts as one entry however many keys it rolls up. When more remain, the response sets @docCodeWord("IsTruncated") and returns a @docCodeWord("NextContinuationToken"); the next request sends it back as @docCodeWord("ContinuationToken") to continue where the last page ended. The token is opaque, not a key.
	}
	@docP() {
		The SDK's paginator runs that loop for you. It sends the previous page's token with each request and stops when a page is not truncated:
	}
	@docCode("listing every page", s3PaginatorCode)
	@docH2("try", "Try it")
	@docSteps(
		"Apply the s3/objects-basics starter and run bb up.",
		"In cmd/objects/main.go, change the list prefix to notes/2026/ and run bb up again.",
		"The common prefix is gone and the two month files appear in Contents.",
		"Remove the Delimiter field and run bb up: every key under the prefix is listed, at any depth.",
	)
}

templ s3Multipart() {
	@docLead() {
		A multipart upload sends one object as numbered parts. Each part is its own request, so a failure costs one part, not the whole upload, and parts can be sent in parallel.
	}
	@docH2("flow", "Create, upload, complete")
	@docP() {
		The starter uploads 11 MiB of random bytes as three parts: 5 MiB, 5 MiB and 1 MiB.
	}
	@docCode("cmd/objects/main.go", s3MultipartCode)
	@docSteps(
		"CreateMultipartUpload starts an upload for the key and returns an upload ID. No object exists yet.",
		"Each UploadPart sends one part with the upload ID and a part number from 1 to 10,000. The server stores it and returns an ETag for that part.",
		"CompleteMultipartUpload sends the list of part numbers and ETags. The server concatenates the parts in ascending part-number order, and only now does the object appear under the key.",
	)
	@docP() {
		The client has to keep the part numbers and ETags itself, which is what the @docCodeWord("parts") slice is for. If an ETag does not match the stored part, Complete fails with @docCodeWord("InvalidPart")&#46;
	}
	@docH2("rules", "Part rules")
	@docP() {
		The S3 API sets these limits on parts:
	}
	@docList(
		"Every part except the last must be at least 5 MiB. A smaller one makes Complete fail with EntityTooSmall.",
		"The last part has no minimum, which is why the starter's 1 MiB tail is fine.",
		"A part can be at most 5 GiB, and an upload can have at most 10,000 parts.",
		"The parts list sent to Complete must be in ascending part-number order, or it fails with InvalidPartOrder.",
	)
	@docH2("retries", "Retrying a part")
	@docP() {
		A part is identified by its upload ID and part number. Uploading the same part number again replaces the earlier part, so retrying a failed part is just sending it again; the parts already stored are untouched. The SDK does this for you on retryable errors: each UploadPart call is a separate request with its own retries, three attempts by default.
	}
	@docH2("incomplete", "Incomplete uploads")
	@docP() {
		In the S3 API an upload has no expiry. Until it is completed or aborted, the server keeps every part, and they take storage even though no object appears in a listing. A crashed uploader leaves them behind. ListMultipartUploads shows what is still in progress, and AbortMultipartUpload deletes an upload's parts:
	}
	@docCode("cleaning up", s3AbortCode)
	@docCallout("Break this") {
		Remove the CompleteMultipartUpload call from the starter and run bb up. The parts are stored, but big/random.bin never appears in a listing until the upload is completed or aborted.
	}
}

templ s3Presigned() {
	@docLead() {
		A presigned URL is a request signed in advance. The signature travels in the query string instead of a header, so anyone holding the URL can make that one request until it expires, with no credentials of their own.
	}
	@docH2("presign", "Presigning a GET")
	@docCode("cmd/objects/main.go", s3PresignCode)
	@docP() {
		@docCodeWord("PresignGetObject") does not contact the server. It builds the same GET request that @docCodeWord("GetObject") would send, signs it with the client's credentials, and returns the URL without sending it. The starter then fetches the URL with @docCodeWord("http.Get"), a client that knows nothing about S3 or signatures, and checks that the bytes match what it uploaded. @docCodeWord("PresignPutObject") works the same way in reverse: your app signs an upload URL and hands it to a browser or caller, which PUTs the file straight to object storage without ever holding keys or sending the bytes through your app.
	}
	@docH2("anatomy", "What is in the URL")
	@docP() {
		This is the URL the SDK produces for the starter's object, split onto lines:
	}
	@docCode("signed.URL", s3PresignedURL)
	@docList(
		"X-Amz-Algorithm: the signing algorithm, SigV4 with HMAC-SHA256.",
		"X-Amz-Credential: the access key id and the scope the key was derived for: date, region, service.",
		"X-Amz-Date: when the URL was signed.",
		"X-Amz-Expires: how many seconds after X-Amz-Date it stays valid; 300 here, from WithPresignExpires(5*time.Minute).",
		"X-Amz-SignedHeaders: the headers covered by the signature. Only host, so the caller can send any other headers.",
		"X-Amz-Signature: the HMAC over the method, path, query parameters and host.",
	)
	@docP() {
		The secret key is not in the URL; only a signature computed with it is. Anyone who has the URL can use it, as often as they like, until it expires, so treat it as a short-lived password for one object.
	}
	@docH2("check", "How the server checks it")
	@docP() {
		The server reads the access key id from @docCodeWord("X-Amz-Credential"), looks up its secret, and recomputes the signature from the request it actually received. Changing the bucket, the key, the method or any signed query parameter, including the expiry, gives a different signature, and the request is refused with @docCodeWord("SignatureDoesNotMatch")&#46; Because the signature covers the GET method and this one key, the URL cannot be turned into an upload or pointed at another object.
	}
	@docH2("expiry", "Expiry")
	@docP() {
		Expiry is checked when the request arrives: the server compares its clock with @docCodeWord("X-Amz-Date") plus @docCodeWord("X-Amz-Expires")&#46; The URL cannot be revoked early except by revoking the key that signed it. If you leave out @docCodeWord("WithPresignExpires"), the SDK uses 900 seconds. The S3 API allows at most seven days.
	}
	@docSteps(
		"Copy the presigned url= line from bb logs objects.",
		"Fetch it from inside the app's container with wget: you get the 11 MiB object.",
		"Change one character of X-Amz-Signature and fetch it again: SignatureDoesNotMatch.",
		"Set WithPresignExpires(10*time.Second), run bb up, wait 15 seconds and fetch the new URL: it has expired.",
	)
}

templ s3InBreadboard() {
	@docLead() {
		What Breadboard runs when you add S3 Compatible, and how it is wired.
	}
	@docH2("server", "SeaweedFS in one container")
	@docP() {
		The service runs the official @docCodeWord("chrislusf/seaweedfs:4.53") image with @docCodeWord("weed server -s3"), which starts SeaweedFS's master, volume server, filer and S3 gateway together. The gateway translates S3 requests into filer operations; each bucket is a folder under @docCodeWord("/buckets") in the filer. Data lives under @docCodeWord("/data") in the container.
	}
	@docP() {
		SeaweedFS is used because MinIO's community edition is archived and no longer publishes images (ADR-0010). The entry is named S3 Compatible, not S3, because it is an object store speaking the S3 API, not Amazon's service. Starters stay within the common subset of the API: buckets, objects, listing, multipart uploads and presigned URLs.
	}
	@docH2("tls", "HTTPS on 8333, nothing else")
	@docP() {
		The S3 gateway is the only network listener: HTTPS on @docCodeWord("s3-compatible.<domain>:8333"), with a certificate issued by your Breadboard CA for that name. The master, volume server and filer bind to 127.0.0.1 inside the container, and the gateway's Iceberg and Lance endpoints are turned off.
	}
	@docP() {
		The gateway also opens a gRPC port that cannot be disabled and binds to the same address. Breadboard mounts a generated @docCodeWord("/etc/seaweedfs/security.toml") that requires mutual TLS on every SeaweedFS gRPC port and, through @docCodeWord("allowed_commonNames"), accepts only the service's own certificate, so other lab certificates are refused. For the same reason there is no plaintext mode: @docCodeWord("tls.mode: off") is rejected for this entry.
	}
	@docH2("keys", "Generated keys and buckets")
	@docP() {
		Breadboard generates one access key pair and passes it to the container as @docCodeWord("AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID") and @docCodeWord("AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY")&#46; With no other identity configured, SeaweedFS uses those variables as a global admin identity, so every app sharing the service uses the same key pair and can reach every bucket.
	}
	@docP() {
		Buckets listed in @docCodeWord("config.buckets") are created at startup. Once the gateway answers, the container's start script checks each bucket with a signed HEAD request and creates it with a signed PUT if it is missing, so restarts leave existing buckets alone. Names are 3 to 63 characters of a-z, 0-9, dots and hyphens, separated by commas with no spaces.
	}
	@docCode("experiment.yaml", s3BucketsYAML)
	@docP() {
		The health check is an authenticated ListBuckets made with @docCodeWord("curl --aws-sigv4") over TLS against the service's name, so the service only reports healthy when TLS, signing and the gateway all work.
	}
	@docH2("env", "What your app receives")
	@docP() {
		Generic names first, for clients that take settings directly, then the variables the official AWS SDKs read on their own:
	}
	@docList(
		"S3_ENDPOINT: https://s3-compatible.<domain>:8333.",
		"S3_REGION: us-east-1, the region requests are signed for.",
		"S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID and S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY: the generated key pair.",
		"S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE: true, for clients that read a path-style setting.",
		"AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_S3, AWS_REGION, AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID, AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY: the same values, under the names config.LoadDefaultConfig reads.",
		"SSL_CERT_FILE: the trust bundle Go uses to verify the gateway.",
	)
	@docH2("explore", "The Buckets tab")
	@docP() {
		The service page has a Buckets tab. It runs a signed ListBuckets from inside the container, then a ListObjectsV2 per bucket, and shows each bucket's object count and total size. It counts the first 1,000 objects of a bucket and marks the count when there are more. You can make the same request yourself:
	}
	@docTerminal(`docker exec bb-s3-lab-s3-compatible-1 sh -c 'curl -s --cacert /etc/bb/ca.pem --aws-sigv4 aws:amz:us-east-1:s3 --user "$AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID:$AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY" https://s3-compatible.bb.internal:8333/'`)
}
